INSERT INTO public.clkr_articles (
  slug_key, locale, title, description, category, reading_time, sections, status, sort_order, translation_group_id, published_at
) VALUES (
  'common-law-union',
  'en',
  'Common-Law Union in Colombia (Unión Marital de Hecho)',
  'What a Colombian common-law union is, how to declare it, when the patrimonial partnership arises, and what changed for adult-only unions under Ley 2447 de 2025.',
  'Family',
  '22 min',
  '[{"id":"definition","title":"I. Legal Definition","html":"<p>Under Colombian civil law, a <strong>common-law union</strong> (<em>unión marital de hecho</em>) is the family community formed by two adults who, without being married to each other, live a <strong>permanent and singular community of life</strong> and are called <em>compañeros permanentes</em>.</p>\n<p>Didactically: (1) it is the statutory label for a stable, exclusive cohabitation project that is not a civil or religious marriage; and (2) it is the gateway institution that can trigger a <strong>presumed patrimonial partnership</strong> (<em>sociedad patrimonial entre compañeros permanentes</em>) after the legal time and capacity thresholds are met.</p>\n<p>The current statutory definition — as amended — requires both partners to be <strong>over 18 years of age</strong>. Early unions involving minors do not produce the civil effects of an adult common-law union; protection routes for children and adolescents apply instead.</p>"},{"id":"legal-framework","title":"II. Legal Framework","html":"<h3>Constitutional</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Constitución Política de Colombia, art. 42</strong> — Family as the basic unit of society; civil and marital equality. In force. Official text (Senado compilation): <a href=\"http://www.secretariasenado.gov.co/senado/basedoc/constitucion_politica_1991.html\">secretariasenado.gov.co — Constitución</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Statutory (Kelsen layer: ley)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ley 54 de 1990</strong> — Defines the <em>unión marital de hecho</em> and the patrimonial regime between permanent partners. In force (as amended). Official: <a href=\"https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=30896\">Función Pública — norma.php?i=30896</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ley 979 de 2005</strong> — Partial reform of Ley 54/1990; agile declaration mechanisms (notarial deed, conciliation minutes, or family-court judgment) for the union and the patrimonial partnership. In force. Official: <a href=\"https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=30898\">Función Pública — norma.php?i=30898</a> · <a href=\"http://www.secretariasenado.gov.co/senado/basedoc/ley_0979_2005.html\">Senado basedoc</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ley 2447 de 2025</strong> — Eliminates early unions; rewrites art. 1 of Ley 54/1990 so the institution exists only between adults 18+, and provides nullity tools for declarations involving minors. In force (promulgated 13 Feb 2025). Official: <a href=\"https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=258236\">Función Pública — norma.php?i=258236</a> · <a href=\"https://www.alcaldiabogota.gov.co/sisjur/normas/Norma1.jsp?i=173637\">Alcaldía de Bogotá SISJUR</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Civil / procedural cross-references</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Código Civil</strong> — Rules on marital partnership liquidation are applied by remisión to the patrimonial partnership (see Ley 54 art. on liquidation). Confirm current article numbers in the consolidated Civil Code before filing.</li>\n<li><strong>Family jurisdiction</strong> — Contested declarations and liquidations are heard by <em>jueces de familia</em> (first instance), under the ordinary means of proof as remitted by Ley 54/979 (historically CPC; today align with the General Code of Process — <em>Código General del Proceso</em>).</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"jurisprudence","title":"III. Jurisprudence","html":"<h3>C-075 de 2007 — Same-sex patrimonial protection</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forum:</strong> Corte Constitucional · <strong>Type:</strong> Constitutional review (C) · <strong>Date:</strong> 7 February 2007 · <strong>Alias:</strong> <em>Same-sex partners enter Ley 54/979</em> · ⏱️ ~45–60 min</li>\n<li><strong>Facts / issue:</strong> Challenge to the heterosexual-only reach of Ley 54 de 1990 as amended by Ley 979 de 2005.</li>\n<li><strong>Holding:</strong> The Court declared the statutes <strong>exequible on the understanding</strong> that the protection regime also applies to same-sex couples who meet the statutory conditions of a permanent and singular community of life (including the two-year threshold for the patrimonial presumption as then framed).</li>\n<li><strong>Ratio:</strong> Excluding same-sex couples from the patrimonial regime was discriminatory and incompatible with dignity and free development of personality.</li>\n<li><strong>Link:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/2007/C-075-07.htm\">corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/2007/C-075-07.htm</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Later constitutional line (functional note)</h3>\n<p>Later decisions have reiterated that expressions such as <em>compañero/compañera permanente</em> must be read consistently with C-075/2007 (e.g. discussion in <strong>C-151 de 2023</strong>). Always open the specific judgment before citing it for a new fact pattern: <a href=\"https://www.corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/2023/c-151-23.htm\">C-151/2023</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Review note:</strong> Supreme Court civil case law on cohabitation-without-shared-roof scenarios (e.g. recent SC lines reported in secondary sources for 2024–2025) should be verified on the Corte Suprema relatoría before reliance — mark as <em>unverified — pending confirmation</em> until the exact radicado PDF is opened.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"id":"core-elements","title":"IV. Core Legal Elements","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Subjects.</strong> Two persons <strong>18 or older</strong>, not married to each other, acting as <em>compañeros permanentes</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Community of life.</strong> A shared life project — mutual assistance, affective and often economic integration, social recognition — not a casual dating relationship.</li>\n<li><strong>Permanence.</strong> Continuity over time; interruptions for work, study, or health can still fit if the project continues (case-specific).</li>\n<li><strong>Singularity.</strong> Exclusivity: the law does not contemplate simultaneous parallel <em>uniones maritales de hecho</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>No marriage between the partners.</strong> The couple is unmarried <em>to each other</em>; prior marriages of either partner affect the <strong>patrimonial</strong> track (see below), not the bare existence of cohabitation.</li>\n<li><strong>Declaration of the union (proof / publicity).</strong> Under Ley 979, existence may be declared by: (i) mutual <strong>escritura pública</strong> before a notary; (ii) <strong>acta de conciliación</strong> at a legally recognized center; or (iii) <strong>family-court judgment</strong> with ordinary evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Patrimonial partnership (<em>sociedad patrimonial</em>).</strong> Presumed and declarable when the statutory time/capacity conditions of Ley 54 art. 2 (as amended by Ley 979) are met — classically <strong>at least two years</strong> of union, with rules on prior marital partnerships that must be dissolved/liquidated before the union’s start in impediment scenarios.</li>\n<li><strong>Assets in the partnership.</strong> As a rule, assets acquired during the union enter the partnership mass; donations, inheritance, and pre-union assets stay out (with nuances on fruits/income — confirm the current art. 3 text after Ley 2447).</li>\n<li><strong>Dissolution &amp; liquidation.</strong> Separation or death dissolves the patrimonial partnership; partners or heirs may seek declaration, dissolution, and liquidation. Contested paths go to family court; agreed paths may use notary/conciliation when the statute allows.</li>\n<li><strong>Minors / early unions.</strong> After Ley 2447/2025, unions involving under-18s do not produce adult UMH effects; restoration-of-rights mechanisms apply; nullity of prior declarations involving minors is available.</li>\n</ol>"},{"id":"doctrinal-note","title":"V. Doctrinal Note","html":"<p>Colombian family doctrine long treated the <em>unión marital de hecho</em> as the legislature’s answer to informal cohabitation: protect the economically weaker partner without forcing marriage. Ley 54/1990 built a <strong>patrimonial</strong> shield; Ley 979/2005 lowered the evidentiary friction by opening notarial and conciliation routes.</p>\n<p>Constitutional jurisprudence then forced the statute beyond its original heterosexual framing. C-075/2007 did not invent a new family form in the Civil Code; it <strong>extended the patrimonial toolkit</strong> so same-sex partners meeting the same permanence and singularity tests could not be left without a property regime.</p>\n<p>The 2025 reform (Ley 2447) shifts the axis again: from “how do we prove cohabitation?” to “who may form a legally effective union at all?” By locking the institution to adults 18+, Congress aligned UMH with the absolute ban on early marriages. For foreign clients, the practical message is blunt: Colombian law will not treat a relationship involving a minor as a property-producing common-law union, and prior paperwork that pretended otherwise is vulnerable to nullity.</p>"},{"id":"examples","title":"VI. Examples","html":"<h3>Example 1 — Expat couple (foreign + Colombian)</h3>\n<p>An American and a Colombian live together in Medellín for three years, exclusive and socially recognized. They execute a mutual notarial deed declaring the <em>unión marital de hecho</em> and the <em>sociedad patrimonial</em>. Later they buy an apartment during the union. On separation, the apartment is presumed partnership property subject to liquidation rules — regardless of whose name appears first on the deed — unless a valid exclusion applies.</p>\n<h3>Example 2 — Common Colombian case</h3>\n<p>Two adults cohabit for 18 months and separate. They may still prove a <em>unión marital de hecho</em> if permanence and singularity are shown, but the <strong>two-year</strong> threshold for the presumed patrimonial partnership is not met. Property division then leans on ordinary civil/commercial titles and proof of contributions, not on Ley 54’s half-share presumption.</p>\n<h3>Example 3 — Edge / special</h3>\n<p>One partner still has an undissolved marital partnership from a prior marriage when cohabitation begins. Even after years together, the path to a <em>sociedad patrimonial</em> is blocked until the statutory sequencing on prior partnerships is satisfied (Ley 54 art. 2 lit. b as amended). Advising “just declare it at the notary” without checking prior marital status is a malpractice trap.</p>"},{"id":"faq","title":"VII. FAQ","html":"<p><strong>1. Is living together enough, or do we need a deed?</strong><br>Cohabitation can exist as a fact. Public declaration (notary, conciliation, or judgment) is how you <strong>prove and operationalize</strong> it for banks, pensions, succession, and litigation.</p>\n<p><strong>2. When does the 50/50 patrimonial presumption start?</strong><br>When the statutory conditions of Ley 54 art. 2 (as amended) are met — typically <strong>two years</strong> of qualifying union, with capacity/impediment rules. The union can exist before the partnership arises.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Do same-sex couples qualify?</strong><br>Yes, under the Constitutional Court’s conditioned exequibility in <strong>C-075 de 2007</strong>, if they meet the same permanence and singularity conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Can we declare the union if one of us is still married to someone else?</strong><br>You may have a factual cohabitation story, but the <strong>patrimonial partnership</strong> rules care deeply about prior marital partnerships. Get a family-law review before signing a notarial package.</p>\n<p><strong>5. What if we never shared the same roof full-time?</strong><br>Some case law explores exceptional non-cohabitation patterns. Treat those holdings as fact-intensive; verify the exact Supreme Court radicado before relying on them.</p>\n<p><strong>6. What changed in 2025?</strong><br><strong>Ley 2447 de 2025</strong> confines legally effective UMH to adults 18+ and strengthens responses to early unions, including nullity of declarations involving minors.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Is this the same as marriage?</strong><br>No. Marriage and UMH are distinct institutions. Effects overlap in places (property, some benefits) but diverge in others (solemnities, certain filiation/status rules, international recognition). Do not assume foreign “common-law marriage” labels map 1:1.</p>"},{"id":"glossary","title":"VIII. Glossary","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Unión marital de hecho</strong> — Colombian statutory common-law union between unmarried adult partners living a permanent, singular community of life.</li>\n<li><strong>Compañero(a) permanente</strong> — Permanent partner in a UMH.</li>\n<li><strong>Sociedad patrimonial</strong> — Presumed property partnership between permanent partners once legal thresholds are met.</li>\n<li><strong>Escritura pública</strong> — Notarial public deed used for mutual declaration.</li>\n<li><strong>Acta de conciliación</strong> — Settlement minutes from a recognized conciliation center.</li>\n<li><strong>Comunidad de vida permanente y singular</strong> — Permanent and exclusive shared life project (core legal test).</li>\n<li><strong>Liquidación</strong> — Settlement and allocation of partnership assets and debts.</li>\n<li><strong>Uniones tempranas</strong> — Early marriages or UMHs involving under-18s (Ley 2447/2025).</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"translation-notes","title":"IX. Translation & Commentaries","html":"<h3>A. Terminological dissonance</h3>\n<p>English “common-law marriage” implies a marriage-equivalent status in some U.S. states. Colombian <em>unión marital de hecho</em> is a <strong>statute-defined cohabitation institution</strong>, not a marriage celebrated without a wedding.</p>\n<h3>B. Comparative legal mapping</h3>\n<p>Unlike many U.S. jurisdictions that abolished common-law marriage, Colombia actively regulates informal unions. Unlike French <em>PACS</em>, UMH can arise from facts and later be formalized; it is not only a registered contract.</p>\n<h3>C. Pragmatic translation choices</h3>\n<p>We keep <em>unión marital de hecho</em>, <em>compañeros permanentes</em>, and <em>sociedad patrimonial</em> in Spanish on first use, then gloss — because English near-equivalents overpromise sameness with marriage.</p>\n<h3>D. Translational insight</h3>\n<p>The hardest term is <em>singular</em>: it means exclusivity of the conjugal project, not “unusual.” Translating it as “unique lifestyle” is a recurring error in machine output.</p>"},{"id":"fun-facts","title":"X. Fun Facts and Curiosities","html":"<ol>\n<li>Ley 54/1990 is older than the 1991 Constitution’s family clause practice that later expanded LGBTQ+ protection — the statute was stretched by the Court before Congress rewrote the age rule in 2025.</li>\n<li>Ley 979/2005 was sold as an “agile proof” reform: notaries and conciliators, not only trials.</li>\n<li>C-075/2007 is still the landmark cite when banks or HR departments ask whether same-sex partners “count” for Ley 54 effects.</li>\n<li>You can have a UMH <strong>without</strong> yet having a <em>sociedad patrimonial</em> if the two-year clock has not run.</li>\n<li>Death of a partner can push liquidation into the succession file — if UMH proof is ready.</li>\n<li>Ley 2447/2025 pairs the UMH age lock with a broader ban on early marriages — one public-policy package.</li>\n<li>Ministry of Justice LegalApp pages still walk citizens through notarial vs judicial declaration paths in plain Spanish (useful client prep, not a substitute for the statute).</li>\n</ol>"},{"id":"bibliography","title":"XI. Bibliography","html":"<ul>\n<li>[Ley · 54/1990 · In force as amended] — Uniones maritales de hecho · ⏱️ ~20 min · 🔗 <a href=\"https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=30896\">https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=30896</a></li>\n<li>[Ley · 979/2005 · In force] — Reforma mecanismos de declaración · ⏱️ ~15 min · 🔗 <a href=\"https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=30898\">https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=30898</a></li>\n<li>[Ley · 2447/2025 · In force] — Eliminación de uniones tempranas · ⏱️ ~25 min · 🔗 <a href=\"https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=258236\">https://www.funcionpublica.gov.co/eva/gestornormativo/norma.php?i=258236</a></li>\n<li>[Sentencia · C-075/2007 · Corte Constitucional] — Extensión a parejas del mismo sexo · ⏱️ ~50 min · 🔗 <a href=\"https://www.corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/2007/C-075-07.htm\">https://www.corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/2007/C-075-07.htm</a></li>\n<li>[Sentencia · C-151/2023 · Corte Constitucional] — Línea interpretativa compañero permanente · ⏱️ ~40 min · 🔗 <a href=\"https://www.corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/2023/c-151-23.htm\">https://www.corteconstitucional.gov.co/relatoria/2023/c-151-23.htm</a></li>\n<li>[Guía · MinJusticia LegalApp] — Declarar convivencia en unión libre · ⏱️ ~10 min · 🔗 <a href=\"https://www.minjusticia.gov.co/programas-co/LegalApp/Paginas/Que-debo-hacer-para-declarar-que-convivo-en-union-libre.aspx\">https://www.minjusticia.gov.co/programas-co/LegalApp/Paginas/Que-debo-hacer-para-declarar-que-convivo-en-union-libre.aspx</a></li>\n</ul>"}]'::jsonb,
